<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arturo Araujo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arturo Araujo was a Salvadoran landowner and engineer who won the free presidential election of 1931 with the support of labour and peasant groups. He took office in March 1931, but his government was overwhelmed by the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> prices in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. In December 1931 a military coup removed him and brought his vice-president, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a>, to power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arturo-araujo.jpg" alt="Arturo Araujo" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arturo_Araujo_Fajardo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arturo Araujo Fajardo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castro-of-santa-trega">Santa Tecla</a> in 1878, the son of Doctor Eugenio Araujo and Enriqueta Fajardo de Araujo, and was a childhood friend of Alfonso Quiñónez Molina, a future president. He took an engineering degree in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and came to admire the British Labour Party. As a landowner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonsonate">Sonsonate</a> he paid his workers twice the average wage and gave them housing and medical care. In 1921 the U.S. chargé d'affaires estimated that he had spent $25,000 on his employees, when the average Salvadoran earned 18 cents a day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Araujo founded the Salvadoran Laborist Party and stood for president in 1919, but Quiñónez, then provisional president, rigged the election for his brother-in-law Jorge Meléndez, and Araujo came third with 1,022 votes. When the government uncovered his plot against Meléndez in March 1920, a shootout broke out at his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. In May 1920 he led a force of between 300 and 1,000 men into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and attacked two outposts at Arcatao, but the army drove them back. Quiñónez let him return in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pio-romero-bosque">Pío Romero Bosque</a>, who took office in 1927, introduced reforms aimed at free and fair elections. In 1931 Araujo came first with 46.65 percent of the vote, and observers considered the election free and fair. He took office on 1 March 1931 with Brigadier General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a> as vice-president and minister of war. Drawing on a nine-point programme proposed by Alberto Masferrer, he promised every Salvadoran food, cloth, work and housing, but corruption and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> worked against him.</p><p>A coup led by junior officers overthrew him in December 1931 and began five decades of military rule, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Araujo died at the Military Hospital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 1 December 1967, aged 88.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arturo Araujo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pío Romero Bosque allowed a free election in 1931, won by <b>Arturo Araujo</b>, but in December 1931 a military coup brought the vice-president, General Maximiliano Hernández Martínez, to power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Caja Seca, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.15,-71.1&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Caja Seca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2587 1822 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arturo Araujo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3037 2092h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3037 2092h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.15,-71.1&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
